In modern MSTest (3.x+), you can inject `TestContext` via the constructor and use `[ClassInitialize]`/`[TestInitialize]` for sync setup and their async equivalents. Here's the recommended pattern:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class MyTests : IAsyncDisposable, IDisposable
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;

    // Constructor injection for TestContext
    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
        // Synchronous per-test setup here
    }

    [ClassInitialize]
    public static void ClassSetup(TestContext context)
    {
        // One-time sync setup for the class
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task TestSetup()
    {
        // Async per-test setup (runs before each test)
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public async Task MyTest()
    {
        _testContext.WriteLine("Running test...");
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task TestTeardown()
    {
        // Async per-test cleanup
    }

    public void Dispose() { /* sync cleanup */ }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync() { /* async cleanup */ }
}
```

**Key points:**
- **Constructor injection** of `TestContext` replaces the old property-setter pattern
- Use `[TestInitialize]` (can be `async Task`) for async per-test setup
- `[ClassInitialize]` handles one-time class-level setup
- Implement `IAsyncDisposable` / `IDisposable` for cleanup as an alternative to `[TestCleanup]`
